      "html": "<ul><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1099",
      "html": "<p>Asia: Japan. Type locality: Southern Japan.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amblycipitidae/liobagrus_reinii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Japan: Honshu, Shikoku and Kyushu Islands. Type locality: Lake Biwa, Japan.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amblycipitidae/liobagrus_reinii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Specimens were captured from under cobbles and boulders, interstices in bedrock, and in clumps of roots of terrestrial vegetation along banks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1099",
      "html": "<p>Yellowish-orange eggs, enclosed in a sticky membrane, are laid inside a cave and guarded by the male. The fry hatch with a large yolk sac still attached and it is best to raise them in fine mesh net suspended in the water column to prevent infection. Once the yolk sac is absorbed, the large fry are greedy eaters and will take any food from prepared flakes and powders to newly hatched brine shrimp.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1099",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: from the Greek leios, meaning smooth, and bagrus, a name usually used for catfishes; in reference to the smooth head. IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2018).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amblycipitidae/liobagrus_reinii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: Dorsal spines (total): 1; dorsal soft rays (total): 6-7; anal spines: 0; anal soft rays: 11-13. Body elongated, anteriorly depressed, posteriorly laterally compressed. Mouth with 4 pairs of barbels. Scales not present on body. Pectoral and dorsal fins with spinous first ray. Adipose fin continuous with caudal fin. Posterior margin of caudal fin rounded.</p><p>Etymology: The specific name reinii honours \"Prof. Rein,\" who collected the type, probably the German geographer Johannes Justus Rein (1835-1918), who explored Japan in 1874-1875.</p><p>IUCN Red List: This species is widespread across Japan with a stable population trend. Therefore, it is assessed as Least Concern (IUCN 2019).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1099",
      "html": "<p>Sitzungsberichte der Gesellschaft Naturforschender Freunde zu Berlin 1878, pp 155.</p>"
    },
    {
